# The 19 "connections" questions (first question of each original guide topic).
CONNECTIONS = {
 "modern-connection-oscillation": ("Why does a moving mass keep going past equilibrium even though the spring pulls it back?", "Because a moving mass tends to keep moving. The spring pulls it back toward equilibrium, but when it gets there it is still moving, so it overshoots and the cycle repeats."),
 "modern-connection-modes": ("Why does adding a third mass give the system another mode?", "Because N masses give N modes. Each mass that can move on its own adds one more way for the whole system to vibrate."),
 "modern-connection-perturbation": ("How can adding mass and stiffness at one spot raise one mode and lower another?", "The same spot can be stretching in one mode and moving in another. Where it stretches, the extra stiffness raises the frequency, and where it moves, the extra mass lowers it."),
 "modern-connection-boundaries": ("Why does a string fixed at both ends have all whole-number harmonics, but a fixed-free system only odd ones?", "A string fixed at both ends must be still at both ends, so any whole number of half-waves fits. A fixed-free system must be still at one end and moving the most at the other, so only an odd number of quarter-waves fits."),
 "modern-connection-test-theory": ("What can a spectrum of a vowel test about the vocal folds?", "It can test whether the harmonics are evenly spaced, which is what the membrane theory predicts. The recordings showed evenly spaced peaks."),
 "modern-connection-air": ("Can air be strongly compressed at a spot where it hardly moves?", "Yes. The air on both sides moves toward and away from that spot, so it is compressed and stretched the most there."),
 "modern-connection-vowels": ("What makes /i/ and /a/ different, without memorizing frequencies?", "The constriction is in a different place. For /i/ it is two-thirds of the way from the glottis, so F1 goes down and F2 goes up. For /a/ it is one-third of the way, so F1 goes up and F2 goes down."),
 "modern-connection-source-filter": ("Does raising your pitch turn /i/ into /a/?", "No. Raising pitch raises F0, which spreads the harmonics apart. The vowel depends on the formants from the vocal tract, so it stays the same."),
 "modern-connection-acoustic-displays": ("How does a spectrogram show a vowel changing?", "Time is across, frequency is up, and darkness is amplitude. Following the dark formant bands over time shows how the vocal tract is moving."),
 "modern-connection-context": ("Why can the vowel from na change what you hear for the m of ma?", "The vowel from na still has the transition from the n, and listeners decide on the consonant during the vowel, so they hear na."),
 "modern-connection-classifier": ("What does the vowel network learn?", "It learns its weights. The weights decide how F1 and F2 lead to the probabilities for the five words."),
 "modern-connection-target": ("Can a prediction add up to 1 and still be wrong?", "Yes. Adding up to 1 only makes it valid. It can still put the most probability on the wrong word, and the desired output shows the right one."),
 "modern-connection-backward": ("Why does backpropagation start at the output instead of the first layer?", "The last weights affect the output directly, so they are easy to judge. Earlier weights affect the output through every later layer, so their answer reuses the later ones, which is dynamic programming."),
 "modern-connection-program": ("What changes if you add a 3-neuron hidden layer right before the 5 outputs?", "The line before it must end in 3, and you add a line that takes 3 and gives 5. The input stays 2, and every line's first number must match the line before it."),
 "modern-connection-nonlinearity": ("Why do the later layers have to be recalculated after the sigmoid changes a hidden value?", "Because the later layers use that value as their input. If it changes, everything after it changes, including the final probabilities."),
 "modern-connection-training-result": ("Runs with different starting weights end at the same accuracy. What does that show?", "Learning ends in the same place even though the paths differ. That accuracy is on the training data, so it does not show how well the network does on new speakers."),
 "modern-connection-sequence": ("How is predicting the next item different from classifying one vector?", "Classifying uses one static vector. Predicting the next item uses the whole sequence so far, so earlier items change what comes next."),
 "modern-connection-phonotactics": ("Why does a good next-letter guesser know English word patterns?", "To guess well, it has to follow the rules for which sounds can come next. Its guesses can only improve by matching English syllables, so it learns them."),
 "modern-connection-architecture": ("How is a critic agent different from a head?", "A head is one expert inside a layer of one LLM. A critic agent is a whole LLM in an agentic system whose job is to check the draft."),
}
